From a93d3a28595af83e036b35ca9eb57f8c60457b2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 06:46:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] Never run both lip-sync passes on one job
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

KOKAO now has two lip-sync passes and nothing stopped both from
running on the same video: the character engine's own per-shot pass
and the optional Studio finishing pass. A character video with both
enabled paid twice and had its mouths redrawn twice.

The character pass wins where both apply, and it is the better of the
two there for reasons of input rather than preference:

  - AUDIO. It syncs from the original narration PCM. The Studio pass
    calls extractNativeAudio on the composed segment, so when a music
    bed is present the model is driven by voice-plus-music, after two
    loudnorm passes and an AAC round trip. These models key off speech
    features; that is a real degradation.
  - SHOT BOUNDARIES. On a character video the Studio plan is a single
    entry spanning the whole runtime, so it is one provider call across
    every cut and dip-to-black. Tracking a face across hard cuts is what
    these models handle worst. The character pass is one call per shot.
  - ENCODE LOSS. The character pass hands the model a purpose-encoded
    clip; the Studio pass re-encodes an already-encoded composite.

None of that applies on Guided Story or the direct engines, where the
optional pass is the only pass — so the predicate is scoped to
topic_to_video with a character source and returns false everywhere
else.

The decision is hoisted above the optional pass's resolution so both
are settled before funding, matching how characterLipSync is already
recorded at enqueue rather than read from a switch mid-render.

Refused rather than silently dropped: the optional pass is a toggle the
user ticked, so the message names the reason and the fix is un-ticking
a box they can see.

The predicate lives in its own module with unit tests rather than
inline in an 11k-line route file, because the route suite cannot be
exercised without object storage.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
